00:06:11.960 --> 00:06:14.390
But Java kind of
running in the JVM

00:06:14.390 --> 00:06:17.893
is a long running instance--
usually pretty hefty workloads.

00:06:17.893 --> 00:06:19.310
How does that
compare to something

00:06:19.310 --> 00:06:21.050
like you just said
in the App Engine

00:06:21.050 --> 00:06:23.370
when you're really working
on it serverlessly?

00:06:23.370 --> 00:06:26.090
RAY TSANG: So I think that
there are many different forms

00:06:26.090 --> 00:06:26.840
of the app.

00:06:26.840 --> 00:06:28.380
It's just another language.

00:06:28.380 --> 00:06:30.560
But certainly, I
think traditionally,

00:06:30.560 --> 00:06:32.360
people may be running
their application

00:06:32.360 --> 00:06:34.220
in a hefty application server.

00:06:34.220 --> 00:06:36.420
Traditionally, a
very long time ago,

00:06:36.420 --> 00:06:40.370
I think my application server
may start in 30 minutes--

00:06:40.370 --> 00:06:43.277
like a whole update process
could be an entire night.

00:06:43.277 --> 00:06:44.610
But that's not the case anymore.

00:06:44.610 --> 00:06:46.460
I mean, it's been
a long time since I

00:06:46.460 --> 00:06:48.300
had to do that, for example.

00:06:48.300 --> 00:06:50.180
The community has
progressed so much now

00:06:50.180 --> 00:06:53.210
that there are a lot of the
microservices frameworks that

00:06:53.210 --> 00:06:56.780
helps you to put up a lot
faster, and focus on the things

00:06:56.780 --> 00:06:57.780
that you want to do.

00:06:57.780 --> 00:07:00.350
So for example, one of the most
popular one this Spring Boot.

00:07:00.350 --> 00:07:01.510
This is pretty well-known.

00:07:01.510 --> 00:07:03.330
It's very, very
popular right now,

00:07:03.330 --> 00:07:06.860
and I see a lot of adoption of
it in production systems too.

00:07:06.860 --> 00:07:10.020
And from a microservices
perspective,

00:07:10.020 --> 00:07:11.650
it gives you
everything you need.

00:07:11.650 --> 00:07:13.280
And you can still
start up your app--

00:07:13.280 --> 00:07:15.630
your services in a
matter of seconds

00:07:15.630 --> 00:07:18.030
as supposed to minutes
for that matter.

00:07:18.030 --> 00:07:19.010
So that really helps.

00:07:19.010 --> 00:07:21.080
And then there are
definitely newer frameworks

00:07:21.080 --> 00:07:23.300
that just came out maybe
this year and last year--

00:07:23.300 --> 00:07:25.210
things like Micronaut
and Quarkus.

00:07:25.210 --> 00:07:27.770
In these new frameworks,
they take on the challenge

00:07:27.770 --> 00:07:29.040
in different ways.

00:07:29.040 --> 00:07:30.580
For example, in
Micronaut, they try

00:07:30.580 --> 00:07:33.770
to generate a lot of the code
at compile time as opposed

00:07:33.770 --> 00:07:37.620
to runtime, and then you can
link them together very easily,

00:07:37.620 --> 00:07:39.860
and then you can just
run them very easily

00:07:39.860 --> 00:07:41.870
without having a lot
of additional overhead

00:07:41.870 --> 00:07:45.340
that you might incur if you want
to generate code at runtime.

00:07:45.340 --> 00:07:48.260
And Quarkus is another
new framework from Red Hat

00:07:48.260 --> 00:07:49.190
primarily.

00:07:49.190 --> 00:07:52.920
And not only do they have
pretty fast start up speed to,

00:07:52.920 --> 00:07:56.180
it allows you to use some of the
well-known standards like JPA,

00:07:56.180 --> 00:07:59.030
and CDI, and a few
other things, or JAX-RS.

00:07:59.030 --> 00:08:01.340
But they also focus
on the ability

00:08:01.340 --> 00:08:04.280
to make sure that you can
potentially compile this

00:08:04.280 --> 00:08:07.470
down into what we
call a native image.

00:08:07.470 --> 00:08:10.490
So today, it's actually
possible to compile

00:08:10.490 --> 00:08:14.120
Java code with Guava native
image down to native binary.

00:08:14.120 --> 00:08:17.360
And you can actually start up
your application potentially

00:08:17.360 --> 00:08:18.627
in sub-second.

00:08:18.627 --> 00:08:20.210
Of course, this is
really, really new.

00:08:20.210 --> 00:08:22.127
I don't know if I would
do this in production,

00:08:22.127 --> 00:08:23.490
but people have tried it.

00:08:23.490 --> 00:08:25.440
And I think all of
the frameworks--

00:08:25.440 --> 00:08:28.380
the ones I mentioned all
have some level of support.

00:08:28.380 --> 00:08:31.070
Micronaut-- since they're
all pretty much generated

00:08:31.070 --> 00:08:32.570
at compile time,
all of these things

00:08:32.570 --> 00:08:35.270
can be compiled down to
native image as well.

00:08:35.270 --> 00:08:38.419
Quarkus has that support by
default. And Spring Boot--

00:08:38.419 --> 00:08:42.159
I was at a conference here at
Devoxx Belgium just this week,

00:08:42.159 --> 00:08:45.250
and I showed a demo of
Spring Boot that was also

00:08:45.250 --> 00:08:47.120
being compiled down
to native image,

00:08:47.120 --> 00:08:49.190
and that was [INAUDIBLE]
sub-second as well.

00:08:49.190 --> 00:08:51.470
So I think the community
is really moving forward.

00:08:51.470 --> 00:08:54.700
But even without this, I
think that couple of seconds,

00:08:54.700 --> 00:08:56.320
I think that's pretty OK.

00:08:56.320 --> 00:08:59.390
Even sometimes I see
heavier workload.

00:08:59.390 --> 00:09:00.940
The promise of the
serverless to me

00:09:00.940 --> 00:09:02.885
is not really just
about the startup speed,

00:09:02.885 --> 00:09:05.010
because in all of these
environments like, say, App

00:09:05.010 --> 00:09:08.180
Engine, you can also specify
minimum instances and the way

00:09:08.180 --> 00:09:09.400
that you want to auto-scale.

00:09:09.400 --> 00:09:12.250
So it's not absolutely
necessary for you to have

00:09:12.250 --> 00:09:13.660
super fast startup time.

00:09:13.660 --> 00:09:16.205
What is key here,
to me, is that you

00:09:16.205 --> 00:09:17.830
don't have to manage
anything, [LAUGHS]

00:09:17.830 --> 00:09:19.720
and that, to me, is really nice.

00:09:19.720 --> 00:09:23.050
So for example,
[INAUDIBLE] the time

00:09:23.050 --> 00:09:25.560
to figure out how to
create a potentially

00:09:25.560 --> 00:09:27.870
compliant infrastructure
that can run your workload.

00:09:27.870 --> 00:09:30.970
And this can take a
month of work just

00:09:30.970 --> 00:09:33.310
to figure out what that
production environment looks

00:09:33.310 --> 00:09:34.970
like, and how that
actually works.

00:09:34.970 --> 00:09:37.730
App Engine is already a
production ready environment,

00:09:37.730 --> 00:09:39.620
and you can just
give us a JAR file,

00:09:39.620 --> 00:09:42.565
which is how most people produce
their application artifacts.

00:09:42.565 --> 00:09:45.010
So you just give us a JAR
and we can just deploy that

00:09:45.010 --> 00:09:47.410
into App Engine, and
everything gets bootstrapped,

00:09:47.410 --> 00:09:48.820
and you just start to serve.

00:09:48.820 --> 00:09:50.095
So that's pretty cool.

00:09:50.095 --> 00:09:50.740
MARK MIRCHANDANI: So
I want to come back

00:09:50.740 --> 00:09:52.823
to some of the points you
actually just mentioned,

00:09:52.823 --> 00:09:55.442
but let's start with
taking a JAR file

00:09:55.442 --> 00:09:56.650
and sending it to App Engine.

00:09:56.650 --> 00:09:58.780
Now the latest
update for App Engine

00:09:58.780 --> 00:10:00.700
is that it supports Java 11.

00:10:00.700 --> 00:10:03.370
First of all, what does that
bring that App Engine didn't

00:10:03.370 --> 00:10:05.365
have for Java before?

00:10:05.365 --> 00:10:07.490
RAY TSANG: So I think there
are two primary things.

00:10:07.490 --> 00:10:10.060
First of all is that it has
the new language support.

00:10:10.060 --> 00:10:13.180
So there is newer language
support in Java 11

00:10:13.180 --> 00:10:15.160
that's not otherwise
there in Java 8.

00:10:15.160 --> 00:10:17.620
But I think the more
interesting perspective

00:10:17.620 --> 00:10:20.470
will be the second part, which
is that traditionally, App

00:10:20.470 --> 00:10:22.480
Engine takes a
WAR file, and that

00:10:22.480 --> 00:10:25.810
is a web application-- a package
where-- you package-- you

00:10:25.810 --> 00:10:28.060
structure your application
differently so that you can

00:10:28.060 --> 00:10:32.290
run this in app server, like
Tomcat or Jetty or something

00:10:32.290 --> 00:10:32.790
else.

00:10:32.790 --> 00:10:37.270
But as the community progresses,
as new frameworks come up

00:10:37.270 --> 00:10:39.070
and are being adopted--
even more popular--

00:10:39.070 --> 00:10:41.320
people don't really want to
configure their app server

00:10:41.320 --> 00:10:44.920
anymore, and they just want to
be the JAR file that just runs.

00:10:44.920 --> 00:10:48.010
So locally, just a Javaless
JAR, and the application

00:10:48.010 --> 00:10:49.660
starts just fine.

00:10:49.660 --> 00:10:52.780
And you don't have to set up
any other additional components

00:10:52.780 --> 00:10:53.800
except for your JVM.

00:10:53.800 --> 00:10:55.370
Then you're up and running.

00:10:55.370 --> 00:10:58.930
So I think in Java 11, we
switched the packaging format

00:10:58.930 --> 00:11:01.540
from WAR to JARs to
serve this use case where

00:11:01.540 --> 00:11:03.290
I do see a lot of people do.

00:11:03.290 --> 00:11:06.230
And you can basically
deploy any JAR file

00:11:06.230 --> 00:11:09.050
now as long as they start
up and listen to the port.

00:11:09.050 --> 00:11:09.350
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Right.

00:11:09.350 --> 00:11:11.683
So now you get a little bit
more flexibility because you

00:11:11.683 --> 00:11:13.420
don't have to have
that web application

00:11:13.420 --> 00:11:16.150
framework around it, plus it
sounds like it's a little bit

00:11:16.150 --> 00:11:19.172
less overhead to get a jar
up and running in App Engine.

00:11:19.172 --> 00:11:20.630
RAY TSANG: It's a
lot easier to do,

00:11:20.630 --> 00:11:22.510
but there's still an
application framework

00:11:22.510 --> 00:11:23.740
that you might want to use.

00:11:23.740 --> 00:11:25.330
You can, of course,
make your application

00:11:25.330 --> 00:11:26.872
without any framework,
but that could

00:11:26.872 --> 00:11:28.640
be potentially a lot of work.

00:11:28.640 --> 00:11:30.320
And of course, if
you do it that way,

00:11:30.320 --> 00:11:32.240
the startup speed is
even going to be faster.

00:11:32.240 --> 00:11:34.720
But if you want the convenience,
I think overwhelmingly,

00:11:34.720 --> 00:11:36.910
people may still choose
to use a framework.

00:11:36.910 --> 00:11:39.100
I think what is really
nice is that now, you

00:11:39.100 --> 00:11:41.050
can use any framework
that you want

00:11:41.050 --> 00:11:43.440
and expecting that
the same result,

00:11:43.440 --> 00:11:46.030
and you don't have to modify
a lot of code-- or any code--

00:11:46.030 --> 00:11:47.950
to be able to run
it in App Engine.

00:11:47.950 --> 00:11:49.960
So for example,
in the past, maybe

00:11:49.960 --> 00:11:53.620
trying to deploy a Spring Boot
application into App Engine

00:11:53.620 --> 00:11:56.110
Java 8, the previous
version, and you

00:11:56.110 --> 00:11:58.230
have to understand
how to convert

00:11:58.230 --> 00:12:01.600
that JAR file that you're
really producing into a WAR file

00:12:01.600 --> 00:12:02.830
with the right structure.

00:12:02.830 --> 00:12:05.380
With Java 11 runtime, you
don't have to do that anymore.

00:12:05.380 --> 00:12:07.940
And I have seen a lot of
people getting into trouble,

00:12:07.940 --> 00:12:10.540
and it's hard to
understand what would it

00:12:10.540 --> 00:12:13.920
take to convert this
package into a package

00:12:13.920 --> 00:12:15.702
that the previous
version can run.

00:12:15.702 --> 00:12:17.410
Now when I say previous
version, I really

00:12:17.410 --> 00:12:19.510
mean that we are actually
having both versions

00:12:19.510 --> 00:12:20.630
running at the same time.

00:12:20.630 --> 00:12:23.890
So if you're on Java 8, and if
you are accustomed to the WAR

00:12:23.890 --> 00:12:26.240
packaging, it continues to
use that, and you should.

00:12:26.240 --> 00:12:28.633
But if you want to try JAR
application deployment,

00:12:28.633 --> 00:12:30.550
then you kind of need
to use the new runtime--

00:12:30.550 --> 00:12:31.717
the Java 11 runtime instead.

00:12:31.717 --> 00:12:33.758
MARK MIRCHANDANI: And like
you mentioned earlier,

00:12:33.758 --> 00:12:35.937
you kind of have your choice
of frameworks, or none,

00:12:35.937 --> 00:12:37.270
if you wanted to do it that way.

00:12:37.270 --> 00:12:39.410
But that obviously
wouldn't be best practices.

00:12:39.410 --> 00:12:40.990
And Spring Boot
was a big framework

00:12:40.990 --> 00:12:43.490
that you kind of mentioned
as a popular example.

00:12:43.490 --> 00:12:45.842
So what does Spring
Boot bring to Java?

00:12:45.842 --> 00:12:48.790
RAY TSANG: So Spring Boot is a
really nice framework where--

00:12:48.790 --> 00:12:51.453
Spring has been around
for a very long time.

00:12:51.453 --> 00:12:52.870
Don't even remember
what year that

00:12:52.870 --> 00:12:54.250
was when they first started.

00:12:54.250 --> 00:12:56.167
TEXT TO SPEECH: Spring
Boot can be traced back

00:12:56.167 --> 00:12:58.330
to its humble
beginnings in 2002.

00:12:58.330 --> 00:13:00.430
RAY TSANG: And traditionally,
it was configured

00:13:00.430 --> 00:13:02.490
through a lot of XML.

00:13:02.490 --> 00:13:05.170
And when Spring Boot came along,
then the other configuration

00:13:05.170 --> 00:13:07.030
became a little
bit more idiomatic,

00:13:07.030 --> 00:13:09.670
and it focused on, I
would say, combination

00:13:09.670 --> 00:13:11.140
over a configuration.

00:13:11.140 --> 00:13:14.650
As opposed to in the past, where
we may end up with a giant XML

00:13:14.650 --> 00:13:17.830
file that just determines
how you can run and serve

00:13:17.830 --> 00:13:21.100
one endpoint, in Spring
Boot, you just create a class

00:13:21.100 --> 00:13:24.070
called a controller, REST
controller [INAUDIBLE] to it.

00:13:24.070 --> 00:13:27.070
And then you create a
method, and add a GET mapping

00:13:27.070 --> 00:13:29.410
or a POST mapping
depending on the operation

00:13:29.410 --> 00:13:31.420
you want to handle,
and off you go.

00:13:31.420 --> 00:13:32.505
It takes maybe--

00:13:32.505 --> 00:13:33.880
I don't know--
five lines of code

00:13:33.880 --> 00:13:36.400
to serve an endpoint
today, and everything

00:13:36.400 --> 00:13:38.075
can get started very easily.

00:13:38.075 --> 00:13:39.700
My good friend, Josh
Long from Pivotal,

00:13:39.700 --> 00:13:41.730
he always shows
how to get started.

00:13:41.730 --> 00:13:44.340
He's got all the best
videos, I think, online,

00:13:44.340 --> 00:13:45.960
teaching people how to do this.

00:13:45.960 --> 00:13:48.720
And his favorite is, of
course, start.spring.io.

00:13:48.720 --> 00:13:52.050
You can go to that site and
you can bootstrap a Spring Boot

00:13:52.050 --> 00:13:53.870
application really,
really easily.

00:13:53.870 --> 00:13:58.440
And you can also add G Suite
to support from start.spring.io

00:13:58.440 --> 00:14:00.693
So in that site,
you can say I want

00:14:00.693 --> 00:14:02.610
to create a web application,
and then you just

00:14:02.610 --> 00:14:04.330
add a web component to it.

00:14:04.330 --> 00:14:06.430
Or you can add a bunch
of other components,

00:14:06.430 --> 00:14:08.430
and it'll just automatically
configure your app.

00:14:08.430 --> 00:14:11.190
You click on Download, and
it generates the zip file,

00:14:11.190 --> 00:14:12.040
and you unpack it.

00:14:12.040 --> 00:14:13.163
You're ready to go.

00:14:13.163 --> 00:14:14.580
MARK MIRCHANDANI:
So that lets you

00:14:14.580 --> 00:14:17.203
start integrating with other
G Suite products a lot easier?

00:14:17.203 --> 00:14:18.870
RAY TSANG: For Spring
Boot specifically,

00:14:18.870 --> 00:14:22.170
we have a project
called Spring Cloud GCP.

00:14:22.170 --> 00:14:24.480
So when you generate
that app, or when

00:14:24.480 --> 00:14:26.700
you're configuring
a Spring Boot app,

00:14:26.700 --> 00:14:29.850
you can add the Spring
Cloud GCP component to it--

00:14:29.850 --> 00:14:32.070
this project has been created
by the cloud Java team

00:14:32.070 --> 00:14:33.280
here in New York.

00:14:33.280 --> 00:14:35.380
And once you add
the starter to it,

00:14:35.380 --> 00:14:38.890
we have a bunch of
integrations with GCP services.

00:14:38.890 --> 00:14:40.560
So if you are already
using Spring Boot,

00:14:40.560 --> 00:14:43.750
you can add this Google Cloud
starter into your application,

00:14:43.750 --> 00:14:45.630
then you can choose
what services

00:14:45.630 --> 00:14:47.230
you want to integrate with.

00:14:47.230 --> 00:14:50.430
So for example, if you only
use something like data store,

00:14:50.430 --> 00:14:53.920
you have the option of
using a store API directly.

00:14:53.920 --> 00:14:56.100
Or if you want
something more close

00:14:56.100 --> 00:14:59.250
to what you are doing with
Spring Data, where you have

00:14:59.250 --> 00:15:03.010
object relational mapping layer
on top of your application

00:15:03.010 --> 00:15:06.360
so you can deal with POJOs,
or Plain Old java Objects,

00:15:06.360 --> 00:15:09.000
and persisting that
into the database,

00:15:09.000 --> 00:15:11.340
with a Spring Data
data store, you

00:15:11.340 --> 00:15:13.230
will be able to do
exactly the same thing.

00:15:13.230 --> 00:15:15.090
And I show this in
my demo quite a bit,

00:15:15.090 --> 00:15:18.180
and the process of using
Spring Data for data store

00:15:18.180 --> 00:15:21.430
is exactly the same as
you would with another SQL

00:15:21.430 --> 00:15:24.900
based data store that's
supported by Spring Data JPA.

00:15:24.900 --> 00:15:27.370
Similarly, we have
support for Spanner too.

00:15:27.370 --> 00:15:29.880
So if you don't want to
use the Spanner raw API,

00:15:29.880 --> 00:15:32.510
then you can use Spring
Data for Spanner,

00:15:32.510 --> 00:15:34.450
and the process is
exactly the same.

00:15:34.450 --> 00:15:37.500
You write the POJO annotated
with the right annotations,

00:15:37.500 --> 00:15:40.500
and you implement the repository
interface, or just extending

00:15:40.500 --> 00:15:41.890
the repository interface.

00:15:41.890 --> 00:15:44.230
And within a few
lines of code, you

00:15:44.230 --> 00:15:47.400
will be able to have a
[INAUDIBLE] access to the data

00:15:47.400 --> 00:15:48.180
store itself.

00:15:48.180 --> 00:15:51.320
So it makes the whole
process very, very easy.

00:15:51.320 --> 00:15:53.070
And [INAUDIBLE]
to data access, I

00:15:53.070 --> 00:15:55.290
think that more
importantly, especially now

00:15:55.290 --> 00:15:57.480
with all a microservices
architecture

00:15:57.480 --> 00:16:00.150
when we're creating more
and more small services

00:16:00.150 --> 00:16:04.975
as can potentially calling
other services as well,

00:16:04.975 --> 00:16:06.600
you kind of need to
know and understand

00:16:06.600 --> 00:16:08.190
how the request kind
of flows through

00:16:08.190 --> 00:16:09.600
from one service to another.

00:16:09.600 --> 00:16:12.952
And for this people, try to
implement a distributed tracer.

00:16:12.952 --> 00:16:14.410
And in Spring Boot,
there's already

00:16:14.410 --> 00:16:16.500
a distributed tracer built in.

00:16:16.500 --> 00:16:19.410
You get this tracer by adding
a starter configuration

00:16:19.410 --> 00:16:21.360
called Spring Cloud Sleuth.

00:16:21.360 --> 00:16:23.490
And as soon as you
add this starter,

00:16:23.490 --> 00:16:25.090
without changing
any of your code,

00:16:25.090 --> 00:16:27.960
you get distributed tracing
across those microservices.

00:16:27.960 --> 00:16:30.990
And for us, for example,
for Spring Cloud GCP,

00:16:30.990 --> 00:16:35.130
you can also add our starter
which will get you-- basically

00:16:35.130 --> 00:16:37.470
uses the same Spring
Cloud Sleuth pipeline,

00:16:37.470 --> 00:16:39.360
or the plumbing of
Spring Cloud Cloud.

00:16:39.360 --> 00:16:41.852
It actually pulls in Spring
Cloud Sleuth for that matter.

00:16:41.852 --> 00:16:43.560
So you configure your
application exactly

00:16:43.560 --> 00:16:46.470
the same way that you
would as any other spring

00:16:46.470 --> 00:16:47.400
cloud application.

00:16:47.400 --> 00:16:49.740
But with our addition,
all the traces

00:16:49.740 --> 00:16:52.350
will automatically flow
to stack driver trace.

00:16:52.350 --> 00:16:54.130
So you don't have
to change any code,

00:16:54.130 --> 00:16:55.920
so your application
becomes pretty much

00:16:55.920 --> 00:16:59.180
agnostic to the environment
that you want to run in,

00:16:59.180 --> 00:17:02.160
or to back end technology that
you want to integrate with.

00:17:02.160 --> 00:17:04.630
Because in all of
these starters,

00:17:04.630 --> 00:17:06.210
when you are adding
capabilities,

00:17:06.210 --> 00:17:09.230
you don't actually have to
change your code in most cases.

00:17:09.230 --> 00:17:10.450
Same thing with logging.

00:17:10.450 --> 00:17:13.353
So if you want to
start driver logging,

00:17:13.353 --> 00:17:14.520
depends on your environment.

00:17:14.520 --> 00:17:17.060
If you are in Kubernetes,
you have two options.

00:17:17.060 --> 00:17:20.400
You just do the regular logger,
which will just be plaintext.

00:17:20.400 --> 00:17:23.619
However, because the log
message is not structured,

00:17:23.619 --> 00:17:26.040
so the logger on the
start driver's side

00:17:26.040 --> 00:17:27.869
wouldn't be able to
parse your message,

00:17:27.869 --> 00:17:30.460
and understand the
different structure.

00:17:30.460 --> 00:17:32.430
So for example, if
you actually add

00:17:32.430 --> 00:17:34.410
Spring Cloud Sleuth
to your application,

00:17:34.410 --> 00:17:36.630
or our starter to
your application,

00:17:36.630 --> 00:17:38.940
every log message
that you print out

00:17:38.940 --> 00:17:41.400
will be associated
with a trace ID,

00:17:41.400 --> 00:17:43.410
because the trace
idea came through

00:17:43.410 --> 00:17:46.230
from another microservice
to yours potentially.

00:17:46.230 --> 00:17:48.540
And you propagate the
same trace ID downstream

00:17:48.540 --> 00:17:49.480
to another service.

00:17:49.480 --> 00:17:51.150
And when you produce
log messages,

00:17:51.150 --> 00:17:54.120
the log lines are actually
associated with the trace ID.

00:17:54.120 --> 00:17:55.912
What that means is that
given the trace ID,

00:17:55.912 --> 00:17:58.037
you could potentially go
back to the login console,

00:17:58.037 --> 00:17:59.250
you can type in the trace ID.

00:17:59.250 --> 00:18:01.590
You can see all the
log messages associated

00:18:01.590 --> 00:18:03.100
with that particular request.

00:18:03.100 --> 00:18:05.440
However, if you just print
it out as plain text,

00:18:05.440 --> 00:18:08.850
there's no way for us to parse
it and put in a special field

00:18:08.850 --> 00:18:10.830
so we can query it better.

00:18:10.830 --> 00:18:14.920
Another mode of doing this is to
output the log as JSON format.

00:18:14.920 --> 00:18:17.580
So you can print your log
message in a structured JSON

00:18:17.580 --> 00:18:20.800
format, and in this JSON
payload there potentially

00:18:20.800 --> 00:18:23.130
is an attribute
called the trace ID,

00:18:23.130 --> 00:18:25.360
and you have to put the
right trace ID in there.

00:18:25.360 --> 00:18:28.370
But that's a lot of work to
do this yourself, so again,

00:18:28.370 --> 00:18:30.660
with this Spring Cloud GCP
integration, what we're

00:18:30.660 --> 00:18:32.380
able to do is to
automatically generate

00:18:32.380 --> 00:18:34.150
this JSON payload for you.

00:18:34.150 --> 00:18:37.193
And when you use the
logger and tracer together,

00:18:37.193 --> 00:18:38.610
the result is
really, really nice.

00:18:38.610 --> 00:18:40.860
What you get is,
for example, you

00:18:40.860 --> 00:18:42.480
can see the distributed trace--

00:18:42.480 --> 00:18:44.400
instead of a trace,
you can see what

00:18:44.400 --> 00:18:47.000
are the services called
in this tree structure.

00:18:47.000 --> 00:18:50.760
And for every one of these span,
for every one of these calls,

00:18:50.760 --> 00:18:53.430
you can say "show
logs," and you actually

00:18:53.430 --> 00:18:55.710
can see the log
messages associated

00:18:55.710 --> 00:18:58.680
with that particular request
for that particular call.

00:18:58.680 --> 00:19:01.200
It's super, super useful
when it comes too--

00:19:01.200 --> 00:19:04.560
you need to troubleshoot some
issues-- you have the trace ID.

00:19:04.560 --> 00:19:07.000
Or you know the issues--
you go back to your console

00:19:07.000 --> 00:19:09.430
and you open up the
request that has the issue,

00:19:09.430 --> 00:19:11.742
and now you can click in and
see exactly what happened.

00:19:11.742 --> 00:19:13.200
MARK MIRCHANDANI:
With Spring Boot,

00:19:13.200 --> 00:19:16.390
it's pretty easy to get a
Java app up and running.

00:19:16.390 --> 00:19:18.090
But really, you
want Spring Cloud

00:19:18.090 --> 00:19:20.280
to take advantage of all
the cloudy atmosphere

00:19:20.280 --> 00:19:24.240
stuff that you can use to
really kind of build, debug,

00:19:24.240 --> 00:19:27.642
and integrate your Java app
with the rest of the Cloud.

00:19:27.642 --> 00:19:28.350
RAY TSANG: Right.

00:19:28.350 --> 00:19:31.000
And when you say Spring Cloud,
I think Spring Cloud in general

00:19:31.000 --> 00:19:33.150
is an umbrella
project from Spring.

00:19:33.150 --> 00:19:35.440
So they include a lot of things.

00:19:35.440 --> 00:19:39.030
And so I think that Spring
Cloud in general, that umbrella

00:19:39.030 --> 00:19:42.420
project helps you to run
applications in a cloud.

00:19:42.420 --> 00:19:44.280
But Spring Cloud
GCP particularly

00:19:44.280 --> 00:19:46.200
gives you the options
of integrating

00:19:46.200 --> 00:19:49.140
a lot of these features directly
with Google Cloud Services.

00:19:49.140 --> 00:19:51.180
And I think the end
result is that when

00:19:51.180 --> 00:19:53.520
you run your application,
you focus on the app.

00:19:53.520 --> 00:19:55.950
When you are ready
to add capabilities,

00:19:55.950 --> 00:19:59.100
you can add these additional
dependencies so that,

00:19:59.100 --> 00:20:01.020
without having to
change a lot of your app

00:20:01.020 --> 00:20:03.030
or without having
to change anything,

00:20:03.030 --> 00:20:06.120
you get these capabilities
added to your app automatically.

00:20:06.120 --> 00:20:09.060
And now you're ready to
leverage the cloud native

00:20:09.060 --> 00:20:11.220
services easier and
faster without having

00:20:11.220 --> 00:20:13.800
to worry about the actual
implementation of that.

00:20:13.800 --> 00:20:15.540
MARK MIRCHANDANI: What if you're
kind of working with an older

00:20:15.540 --> 00:20:18.210
Java application, and you want
to migrate that to the cloud

00:20:18.210 --> 00:20:18.960
instead?

00:20:18.960 --> 00:20:20.877
So if you're using like
Hibernate or something

00:20:20.877 --> 00:20:23.700
of that nature, what changes do
you need to make to get that up

00:20:23.700 --> 00:20:24.587
and running?

00:20:24.587 --> 00:20:26.920
RAY TSANG: So Hibernate has
been around for a long time,

00:20:26.920 --> 00:20:30.000
and it's still being used a lot,
so it's not necessarily older.

00:20:30.000 --> 00:20:32.370
But everybody who is
using relational database

00:20:32.370 --> 00:20:34.140
is probably going to
be using Hibernate

00:20:34.140 --> 00:20:35.790
somewhere in their
stack, or maybe

00:20:35.790 --> 00:20:37.260
they just use JDBC driver.

00:20:37.260 --> 00:20:39.900
But you can continue to
use hibernate, of course,

00:20:39.900 --> 00:20:41.190
talking to our databases.

00:20:41.190 --> 00:20:45.160
For example, we can talk to
our SQL databases on Cloud SQL.

00:20:45.160 --> 00:20:47.160
We support Postgres
in MySQL, so that

00:20:47.160 --> 00:20:49.270
should be pretty easy
to get started with.

00:20:49.270 --> 00:20:51.990
But if you want to adopt
something like Cloud Spanner,

00:20:51.990 --> 00:20:53.580
it is a relational
database that kind

00:20:53.580 --> 00:20:55.680
of spans across
multiple regions,

00:20:55.680 --> 00:20:58.123
and potentially across the
world with consistency,

00:20:58.123 --> 00:20:59.290
which is absolutely amazing.

00:20:59.290 --> 00:21:02.330
But to use Spanner, you
have a couple of choices.

00:21:02.330 --> 00:21:05.430
Just recently, they have
released an open source JDBC

00:21:05.430 --> 00:21:09.540
driver, where you can just use
the regular JDBC connections,

00:21:09.540 --> 00:21:12.363
and you can do read and write
operations through JDBC.

00:21:12.363 --> 00:21:14.280
However, if you want to
use it with Hibernate,

00:21:14.280 --> 00:21:16.113
what is special about
this is that Hibernate

00:21:16.113 --> 00:21:19.030
will need to understand how
to talk to the backend SQL

00:21:19.030 --> 00:21:21.510
databases, because every SQL
database may have a slightly

00:21:21.510 --> 00:21:23.370
different way of communicating.

00:21:23.370 --> 00:21:24.960
They have different
SQL dialects.

00:21:24.960 --> 00:21:28.920
So the team actually has
created a Hibernate dialect

00:21:28.920 --> 00:21:29.630
for Spanner.

00:21:29.630 --> 00:21:31.590
And at the time of
this recording--

00:21:31.590 --> 00:21:32.550
this is still beta--

00:21:32.550 --> 00:21:34.060
I don't know when they
are going to [INAUDIBLE]..

00:21:34.060 --> 00:21:35.190
I heard it's going
to be pretty soon,

00:21:35.190 --> 00:21:36.540
so it's definitely coming soon.

00:21:36.540 --> 00:21:37.830
But at the time of the
recording right now,

00:21:37.830 --> 00:21:39.500
you can actually give it a try--

00:21:39.500 --> 00:21:40.570
that's in beta right now.

00:21:40.570 --> 00:21:43.080
So what that means is
that it's not just there

00:21:43.080 --> 00:21:44.650
to work with
existing applications

00:21:44.650 --> 00:21:47.850
that's using Hibernate, but also
things like running anything

00:21:47.850 --> 00:21:51.090
with micro profile that
uses JPA that uses Hibernate

00:21:51.090 --> 00:21:51.930
behind the scenes.

00:21:51.930 --> 00:21:54.060
You can actually integrate
that with Cloud Spanner

00:21:54.060 --> 00:21:57.450
too to take advantage of
this really awesome database

00:21:57.450 --> 00:21:59.340
architecture that Google has.

00:21:59.340 --> 00:22:01.710
Or if you are using
something like Quarkus,

00:22:01.710 --> 00:22:03.990
you can also use
Quarkus with JPA

00:22:03.990 --> 00:22:05.830
with Hibernate
behind the scenes,

00:22:05.830 --> 00:22:08.400
and then you can use the
Hibernate dialect for Spanner

00:22:08.400 --> 00:22:11.100
to communicate and do cloud
operations with Spanner

00:22:11.100 --> 00:22:12.040
as well.

00:22:12.040 --> 00:22:12.720
MARK MIRCHANDANI:
So regardless of the

00:22:12.720 --> 00:22:14.345
frameworks that you're
using, you still

00:22:14.345 --> 00:22:17.640
have a good way to access
basically Google's databases,

00:22:17.640 --> 00:22:20.370
but still being able
to run your Java code

00:22:20.370 --> 00:22:21.690
in whatever way you mentioned.

00:22:21.690 --> 00:22:24.240
Like you said, Kubernetes, you
can run it on an App Engine,

00:22:24.240 --> 00:22:26.460
you can run it in a more
traditional Compute Engine

00:22:26.460 --> 00:22:27.280
instance.

00:22:27.280 --> 00:22:28.577
You have all those options.

00:22:28.577 --> 00:22:30.160
RAY TSANG: Or even
if you are on-prem.

00:22:30.160 --> 00:22:32.785
You can run it anywhere because,
I think, all of these services

00:22:32.785 --> 00:22:33.790
are just APIs.

00:22:33.790 --> 00:22:36.660
So if you have a fast
enough internet, [LAUGHS]

00:22:36.660 --> 00:22:38.790
you should be able to run
it anywhere that you want

00:22:38.790 --> 00:22:41.250
and still be able to
authenticate and get access

00:22:41.250 --> 00:22:43.380
to the database
that you want to.

00:22:43.380 --> 00:22:44.340
MARK MIRCHANDANI: But
one of the nice points

00:22:44.340 --> 00:22:46.440
you mentioned is that by
putting it in the cloud,

00:22:46.440 --> 00:22:47.730
you're also able
to take advantage

00:22:47.730 --> 00:22:49.855
of all of the ops things
you can do with it, right?

00:22:49.855 --> 00:22:51.900
Logging, monitoring,
debugging, tracing,

00:22:51.900 --> 00:22:54.540
all these things that
are not as easily done

00:22:54.540 --> 00:22:55.797
in some environments.

00:22:55.797 --> 00:22:56.880
RAY TSANG: Oh, absolutely.

00:22:56.880 --> 00:22:59.190
I think GCP Next,
San Francisco, I

00:22:59.190 --> 00:23:02.680
did a talk that kind of showed
how everything works together.

00:23:02.680 --> 00:23:04.800
So in the talk, I
showed how do you

00:23:04.800 --> 00:23:07.460
get started with Spring
Cloud GCP with Spring Boot,

00:23:07.460 --> 00:23:10.410
and how do you add some of
these data access options

00:23:10.410 --> 00:23:12.510
with Spring Data,
Spanner with data store.

00:23:12.510 --> 00:23:14.490
And then you can add
trace, and logging,

00:23:14.490 --> 00:23:17.010
and a bunch of other
capabilities to your app.

00:23:17.010 --> 00:23:18.900
And then it's just a
matter of picking what

00:23:18.900 --> 00:23:20.160
the runtime environment is.

00:23:20.160 --> 00:23:22.500
And you can containerize
your app, for example.

00:23:22.500 --> 00:23:26.220
And the team also have
created something called JIB--

00:23:26.220 --> 00:23:29.640
J-I-B. And this is a really,
really easy way, for example,

00:23:29.640 --> 00:23:31.500
to containerize your
Java application,

00:23:31.500 --> 00:23:33.690
producing a container
image without you

00:23:33.690 --> 00:23:35.375
having to write a Docker file.

00:23:35.375 --> 00:23:36.750
After you save
your Docker file--

00:23:36.750 --> 00:23:40.148
everyone can write Docker files,
but to write it correctly,

00:23:40.148 --> 00:23:41.440
it can be somewhat challenging.

00:23:41.440 --> 00:23:43.300
You have to follow
many best practices.

00:23:43.300 --> 00:23:46.110
So with JIB, we actually
implemented some of these best

00:23:46.110 --> 00:23:49.500
practices, and they can produce
a very efficient container

00:23:49.500 --> 00:23:53.190
image without you having to ever
write a line of Docker file.

00:23:53.190 --> 00:23:56.700
And then you can push this
image to our Container Registry,

00:23:56.700 --> 00:23:59.940
or another registry, whichever
that you need to push to,

00:23:59.940 --> 00:24:02.730
and you can deploy that
container to the Kubernetes

00:24:02.730 --> 00:24:04.825
environment or a run
environment for that matter.

00:24:04.825 --> 00:24:07.200
MARK MIRCHANDANI: And you can
integrate JIB with whatever

00:24:07.200 --> 00:24:08.490
your build process is, right?

00:24:08.490 --> 00:24:10.782
So if you're using like
Gradle, or Maven, or something,

00:24:10.782 --> 00:24:13.320
it's very easy to just
containerize it and then ship

00:24:13.320 --> 00:24:13.830
it.

00:24:13.830 --> 00:24:14.310
RAY TSANG: That's right.

00:24:14.310 --> 00:24:16.090
Yeah, JIB supports
both Maven and Gradle,

00:24:16.090 --> 00:24:18.315
two of the most popular
build tools right now.

00:24:18.315 --> 00:24:20.190
MARK MIRCHANDANI: So
from code to deployment,

00:24:20.190 --> 00:24:22.020
it sounds like there's
kind of integration

00:24:22.020 --> 00:24:25.440
points throughout the entire
stack of Java development

00:24:25.440 --> 00:24:27.930
that kind of are addressed
by different parts of Google

00:24:27.930 --> 00:24:28.500
Cloud.

00:24:28.500 --> 00:24:29.730
We just talked about
the build, we just

00:24:29.730 --> 00:24:31.563
talked about the framework
that's supported,

00:24:31.563 --> 00:24:33.420
we talked about the
kind of operations piece

00:24:33.420 --> 00:24:34.740
with logging and monitoring.

00:24:34.740 --> 00:24:36.270
What about the IDE?

00:24:36.270 --> 00:24:38.970
RAY TSANG: I think that
recently, the team has released

00:24:38.970 --> 00:24:41.710
Cloud Code, and Cloud
Code is the plug-in

00:24:41.710 --> 00:24:45.140
that you can add to your IDE,
specifically IntelliJ or VS

00:24:45.140 --> 00:24:45.840
Code.

00:24:45.840 --> 00:24:49.410
So for example, in the IDE, you
can create a new Spring Boot

00:24:49.410 --> 00:24:50.230
application.

00:24:50.230 --> 00:24:52.050
And from the IDE,
you can potentially

00:24:52.050 --> 00:24:53.160
add App Engine support.

00:24:53.160 --> 00:24:55.380
So directly from
the IDE, you will

00:24:55.380 --> 00:24:58.110
be able to add whatever
is necessary to deploy

00:24:58.110 --> 00:25:01.290
to App Engine without ever
having to go back to the shell.

00:25:01.290 --> 00:25:03.330
And Cloud Code also
has the capability

00:25:03.330 --> 00:25:05.160
of connecting to a
Kubernetes cluster.

00:25:05.160 --> 00:25:09.140
So from Cloud Code, they give
you quite a few really nice

00:25:09.140 --> 00:25:10.653
autocompletions
and everything, so

00:25:10.653 --> 00:25:12.570
that when you're editing
the YAML files that's

00:25:12.570 --> 00:25:15.572
specific to Google Cloud,
you can get autocompletion,

00:25:15.572 --> 00:25:16.530
which is super awesome.

00:25:16.530 --> 00:25:19.710
I show this in my presentations,
in my talks all the time.

00:25:19.710 --> 00:25:23.310
And who remembers how to write
complicated YAML file, right?

00:25:23.310 --> 00:25:26.100
But with some editor support,
it becomes fairly easy.

00:25:26.100 --> 00:25:28.110
And potentially
from the IDE, what

00:25:28.110 --> 00:25:31.520
you can do is to just Bootstrap
a brand new application

00:25:31.520 --> 00:25:34.740
and then it actually has a
builtin template that shows you

00:25:34.740 --> 00:25:36.930
how to create a Spring
Boot application

00:25:36.930 --> 00:25:39.510
with the necessary Kubernetes
components or Kubernetes

00:25:39.510 --> 00:25:43.110
files that you need to take
advantage of this plugin.

00:25:43.110 --> 00:25:46.510
So for example, in a typical
Kubernetes development cycle,

00:25:46.510 --> 00:25:47.850
you will need to create the app.

00:25:47.850 --> 00:25:51.090
You need to use JIB to
create the container image,

00:25:51.090 --> 00:25:53.360
and then you have to
write your Kubernetes

00:25:53.360 --> 00:25:56.430
manifest-- the deployment
YAML files, for example.

00:25:56.430 --> 00:25:58.290
And then if you
change some code,

00:25:58.290 --> 00:26:01.370
you have to recreate the image,
and then potentially update

00:26:01.370 --> 00:26:04.650
the manifest, and then re-push
everything to a Kubernetes

00:26:04.650 --> 00:26:07.020
environment, whether it's
on your machine minikube,

00:26:07.020 --> 00:26:09.630
or remotely in a
Kubernetes cluster.

00:26:09.630 --> 00:26:11.740
And then you maybe test
it out a little bit,

00:26:11.740 --> 00:26:12.870
and then if you
find another issue,

00:26:12.870 --> 00:26:14.290
you have to redo
the cycle again.

00:26:14.290 --> 00:26:16.740
And there's a really nice
tool called Scaffold.

00:26:16.740 --> 00:26:18.330
It's a command
line tool that you

00:26:18.330 --> 00:26:22.140
can use that basically automates
this development cycle for you

00:26:22.140 --> 00:26:24.630
so that whenever you
change some code,

00:26:24.630 --> 00:26:26.822
it can automatically
refresh the image,

00:26:26.822 --> 00:26:28.530
and then automatically
push in that image

00:26:28.530 --> 00:26:31.410
to the right registry, and
then automatically redeploy

00:26:31.410 --> 00:26:32.750
the application for you.

00:26:32.750 --> 00:26:34.680
And from your console,
from your computer,

00:26:34.680 --> 00:26:37.650
without having to connect
remotely yourself,

00:26:37.650 --> 00:26:39.570
it will actually
output a lock for you.

00:26:39.570 --> 00:26:41.460
So you feels like
you're running your app

00:26:41.460 --> 00:26:43.020
locally even though
it's actually

00:26:43.020 --> 00:26:46.110
running in a remote container in
a Kubernetes cluster somewhere

00:26:46.110 --> 00:26:47.190
or in your minikube.

00:26:47.190 --> 00:26:50.430
And Cloud Code can integrate
with Scaffold really well.

00:26:50.430 --> 00:26:52.470
So if you add Scaffold
to your application,

00:26:52.470 --> 00:26:55.290
your IDE will say, hey, do you
want to add scaffold support?

00:26:55.290 --> 00:26:58.920
And in IntelliJ, for example,
it will add a little Run button

00:26:58.920 --> 00:27:00.630
that's specifically
designed to run

00:27:00.630 --> 00:27:02.810
in Kubernetes in a
continuous deployment mode.

00:27:02.810 --> 00:27:05.460
And you continue your
regular editing cycle,

00:27:05.460 --> 00:27:08.820
and once you save the file,
it will recompile and recreate

00:27:08.820 --> 00:27:10.512
the image, and
redeploy it for you.

00:27:10.512 --> 00:27:11.220
It's really neat.

00:27:11.220 --> 00:27:14.070
And the team actually
went even a step further

00:27:14.070 --> 00:27:16.020
by adding the
debugging support too.

00:27:16.020 --> 00:27:18.300
So like if we run
applications locally,

00:27:18.300 --> 00:27:20.022
we can attach with
debugger so that you

00:27:20.022 --> 00:27:21.480
can step through
the code-- you can

00:27:21.480 --> 00:27:23.250
add a breakpoint in your code.

00:27:23.250 --> 00:27:24.780
So you can add a
debugging point,

00:27:24.780 --> 00:27:26.730
and you can step through the
code to see what's going on.

00:27:26.730 --> 00:27:28.880
If you want to do that in
a Kubernetes environment

00:27:28.880 --> 00:27:30.390
during development
time, of course,

00:27:30.390 --> 00:27:34.410
then you can use Cloud Code with
Scaffold in the debugging mode.

00:27:34.410 --> 00:27:36.270
And what that will
do is automatically

00:27:36.270 --> 00:27:38.190
enable debugging
support in your app,

00:27:38.190 --> 00:27:40.290
and automatically
attach the debugger

00:27:40.290 --> 00:27:43.900
to this remote process that's
running a Kubernetes cluster.

00:27:43.900 --> 00:27:46.762
And in your IDE, you'll be
able to debug this application

00:27:46.762 --> 00:27:48.720
even if it's not running
on your local machine.

00:27:48.720 --> 00:27:50.370
And this whole
process is seamless,

00:27:50.370 --> 00:27:51.860
which is super, super neat.

00:27:51.860 --> 00:27:53.370
MARK MIRCHANDANI: A great
place for people to get started

00:27:53.370 --> 00:27:56.010
would be Cloud Code, because
all of the integrations

00:27:56.010 --> 00:27:58.950
there really make the
entire development

00:27:58.950 --> 00:28:00.683
process a lot easier.

00:28:00.683 --> 00:28:01.350
RAY TSANG: Yeah.

00:28:01.350 --> 00:28:03.870
So for example, if you want
to also add Google Cloud

00:28:03.870 --> 00:28:06.630
Services, even not using Spring,
even not using Spring Cloud

00:28:06.630 --> 00:28:10.020
GCP, and you want to add some
client libraries in Cloud Code,

00:28:10.020 --> 00:28:13.530
there is also a option for
you to connect to a service.

00:28:13.530 --> 00:28:16.510
And from the UI, it will
allow you to determine

00:28:16.510 --> 00:28:17.980
what service you want to use.

00:28:17.980 --> 00:28:20.970
And once you select it, it will
enable the API for you which

00:28:20.970 --> 00:28:22.510
a lot of people forget to do.

00:28:22.510 --> 00:28:24.240
If you want to use
any API services,

00:28:24.240 --> 00:28:25.835
you have to go enable the API.

00:28:25.835 --> 00:28:28.460
The IDE will actually tell you,
do you want to enable this API?

00:28:28.460 --> 00:28:31.362
It will generate the service
account key for you as well,

00:28:31.362 --> 00:28:33.570
and then it will automatically
add the client library

00:28:33.570 --> 00:28:35.535
to your Maven or Gradle build.

00:28:35.535 --> 00:28:38.370
So you can very easily add
additional capabilities

00:28:38.370 --> 00:28:39.420
to your application.

00:28:39.420 --> 00:28:40.440
MARK MIRCHANDANI: That
sounds pretty impressive,

00:28:40.440 --> 00:28:42.740
because It sounds like
it really is designed

00:28:42.740 --> 00:28:44.480
to take care of as
much as possible

00:28:44.480 --> 00:28:47.150
for you, because it wants
to get you to the point

00:28:47.150 --> 00:28:48.950
where you're just
worrying about the code.

00:28:48.950 --> 00:28:50.492
All of the pipelines,
all of the work

00:28:50.492 --> 00:28:53.540
that you need to do, and even
as much as integrating basically

00:28:53.540 --> 00:28:54.990
the remote debugging.

00:28:54.990 --> 00:28:56.023
All of it's right there.

00:28:56.023 --> 00:28:56.690
RAY TSANG: Yeah.

00:28:56.690 --> 00:28:58.897
And of course, since
you mentioned debugging,

00:28:58.897 --> 00:29:00.980
I think there's another
thing that a lot of people

00:29:00.980 --> 00:29:01.790
don't know about.

00:29:01.790 --> 00:29:02.790
I do this all the time--

00:29:02.790 --> 00:29:04.700
I ask people who
are brave enough

00:29:04.700 --> 00:29:07.400
to attach a real debugger
to your production workload,

00:29:07.400 --> 00:29:10.638
and debug your application
live, [LAUGHS] or even

00:29:10.638 --> 00:29:11.680
your staging environment?

00:29:11.680 --> 00:29:13.400
It will be pretty
difficult to do, mostly

00:29:13.400 --> 00:29:14.912
because when the
requests come in,

00:29:14.912 --> 00:29:17.120
people have to wait for you
to step through the code.

00:29:17.120 --> 00:29:20.010
So on Google Cloud, actually,
you have another debugger,

00:29:20.010 --> 00:29:21.290
it's called Cloud Debugger.

00:29:21.290 --> 00:29:23.840
And the difference there is,
for Cloud Debugger, what you do

00:29:23.840 --> 00:29:26.360
is you add a Cloud Debugger
agent to your application,

00:29:26.360 --> 00:29:28.010
and it will
automatically connect

00:29:28.010 --> 00:29:29.990
to our debugger console.

00:29:29.990 --> 00:29:31.930
And from there in
the console, you

00:29:31.930 --> 00:29:33.680
can potentially
upload your code,

00:29:33.680 --> 00:29:36.410
or see your code already if
it's running in a Git repository

00:29:36.410 --> 00:29:39.020
that we have access to if
you give us the permission

00:29:39.020 --> 00:29:39.740
to do it.

00:29:39.740 --> 00:29:41.120
We can automatically
pull down the code,

00:29:41.120 --> 00:29:43.412
or you can just upload the
code that you want to debug.

00:29:43.412 --> 00:29:47.150
And in that console, you can
add a snapshot point as opposed

00:29:47.150 --> 00:29:48.690
to a breakpoint.

00:29:48.690 --> 00:29:50.620
And as somebody flows
through that flow

00:29:50.620 --> 00:29:52.100
that you're trying
to debug, we'll

00:29:52.100 --> 00:29:56.220
capture a snapshot of the
state of that moment in time.

00:29:56.220 --> 00:29:59.510
And then you can inspect
the values and potentially

00:29:59.510 --> 00:30:01.850
the code stack of
what actually happened

00:30:01.850 --> 00:30:03.320
without stopping the world.

00:30:03.320 --> 00:30:05.780
So the user is not
being affected by this,

00:30:05.780 --> 00:30:08.360
but you are still able
to see the relevant data

00:30:08.360 --> 00:30:08.970
that you need.

00:30:08.970 --> 00:30:11.820
And what's funny is that I
did a joint talk with Robert

00:30:11.820 --> 00:30:13.610
[INAUDIBLE] another cloud DA.

00:30:13.610 --> 00:30:16.310
He actually focuses on Spanner,
but we did a troubleshooting

00:30:16.310 --> 00:30:18.170
talk in the past, and
he made a joke like,

00:30:18.170 --> 00:30:19.790
if you really want to
troubleshoot the production

00:30:19.790 --> 00:30:21.540
environment-- this
is how he does it.

00:30:21.540 --> 00:30:23.780
He does it binary,
log, debugging.

00:30:23.780 --> 00:30:25.120
So what he does--

00:30:25.120 --> 00:30:26.840
and I think I do
the same thing too--

00:30:26.840 --> 00:30:29.070
is if something really
doesn't work out,

00:30:29.070 --> 00:30:31.190
you need to see what
is actually happening.

00:30:31.190 --> 00:30:33.392
You probably want to add
a log message somewhere,

00:30:33.392 --> 00:30:34.850
because in the app,
they'll usually

00:30:34.850 --> 00:30:36.142
have all the logs that we need.

00:30:36.142 --> 00:30:38.360
But then you add a log to
the top at the beginning,

00:30:38.360 --> 00:30:40.070
and you add the log
to the very end.

00:30:40.070 --> 00:30:42.620
And the code doesn't
reach the very end,

00:30:42.620 --> 00:30:44.240
you add a log
[INAUDIBLE] between.

00:30:44.240 --> 00:30:45.470
[LAUGHS]

00:30:45.470 --> 00:30:48.290
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Very
familiar with that method.

00:30:48.290 --> 00:30:50.505
Print one, print two,
print three, print four,

00:30:50.505 --> 00:30:52.880
and then you're just looking
through streams of numbers--

00:30:52.880 --> 00:30:55.490
like, oh, I see, the
code stopped at 37,

00:30:55.490 --> 00:30:56.480
so let's work there.

00:30:56.480 --> 00:30:57.897
RAY TSANG: Yeah,
[LAUGHS] exactly.

00:30:57.897 --> 00:31:00.380
However, if you
had to figure out

00:31:00.380 --> 00:31:02.840
where to add all these
37 things to log,

00:31:02.840 --> 00:31:04.640
every time you
change the code, you

00:31:04.640 --> 00:31:06.020
have to do another deployment.

00:31:06.020 --> 00:31:08.330
If you're lucky, you can
get it through in minutes.

00:31:08.330 --> 00:31:10.190
But I think in most
of the systems,

00:31:10.190 --> 00:31:13.380
you could have to wait hours
before anything gets deployed.

00:31:13.380 --> 00:31:16.520
So with a debugger, you can
also add log statements.

00:31:16.520 --> 00:31:20.510
And it will at log statements
to your application in real time

00:31:20.510 --> 00:31:22.790
without you having to do
anything additionally.

00:31:22.790 --> 00:31:25.230
So you don't have to redeploy
or anything like that.

00:31:25.230 --> 00:31:28.310
And it also does not affect
the state of your app.

00:31:28.310 --> 00:31:30.200
We're not actually
modifying your code,

00:31:30.200 --> 00:31:32.228
we're also not modifying
your application.

00:31:32.228 --> 00:31:33.770
We're just adding
these interceptors,

00:31:33.770 --> 00:31:35.413
these hooks, so
that we know when

00:31:35.413 --> 00:31:36.830
you pass through
certain lines, we

00:31:36.830 --> 00:31:39.230
will print out some
messages for you.

00:31:39.230 --> 00:31:41.593
So pretty awesome tool that
very few people know about.

00:31:41.593 --> 00:31:43.010
MARK MIRCHANDANI:
Sounds like it's

00:31:43.010 --> 00:31:46.040
an amazing way to actually debug
in our production environment

00:31:46.040 --> 00:31:48.112
with very minimal
impact to the user.

00:31:48.112 --> 00:31:48.820
RAY TSANG: Right.

00:31:48.820 --> 00:31:51.510
[INAUDIBLE] because in a
production environment,

00:31:51.510 --> 00:31:54.440
most people may not
actually want anybody

00:31:54.440 --> 00:31:57.050
to just go in and print
out anything they want,

00:31:57.050 --> 00:31:58.670
or to see any of the data.

00:31:58.670 --> 00:32:01.410
So I think that's
a real concern.

00:32:01.410 --> 00:32:04.130
So this is really just
for break glass type

00:32:04.130 --> 00:32:07.130
of scenario for specially
authorized users

00:32:07.130 --> 00:32:08.540
to be able to do this.

00:32:08.540 --> 00:32:10.720
And of course, you can
lock down who can do this.

00:32:10.720 --> 00:32:13.090
You can disable this
by default, and only

00:32:13.090 --> 00:32:14.540
when you absolutely need--

00:32:14.540 --> 00:32:16.160
actually, I think
better use case

00:32:16.160 --> 00:32:19.130
is probably like even staging,
or some higher environment

00:32:19.130 --> 00:32:20.510
before you can
get to production.

00:32:20.510 --> 00:32:23.630
During the QA tests
or the UAT tests,

00:32:23.630 --> 00:32:25.400
some weird issue occurred.

00:32:25.400 --> 00:32:27.890
Of course, we always say
it works on my machine,

00:32:27.890 --> 00:32:30.130
but you never know
what the user entered.

00:32:30.130 --> 00:32:32.960
And in a staging environment,
whatever that UAT tester may

00:32:32.960 --> 00:32:35.840
have done, it will be
very easy to understand

00:32:35.840 --> 00:32:36.813
what actually happened.

00:32:36.813 --> 00:32:39.230
And I think a lot of people
are kind of afraid to get this

00:32:39.230 --> 00:32:41.210
into production in most cases.

00:32:41.210 --> 00:32:44.100
But everything can be locked
down, so that's the good news.

00:32:44.100 --> 00:32:45.058
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Yeah.

00:32:45.058 --> 00:32:47.600
I'm convinced that if you just
label a server "production,"

00:32:47.600 --> 00:32:49.610
that automatically makes
it buggier somehow.

00:32:49.610 --> 00:32:49.910
[LAUGHTRACK]

00:32:49.910 --> 00:32:50.577
RAY TSANG: Yeah.

00:32:50.577 --> 00:32:54.080
[LAUGHS] You just get to see
all the scenarios that you never

00:32:54.080 --> 00:32:55.038
saw before.

00:32:55.038 --> 00:32:57.080
Who'd have thought this
is what people have done?

00:32:57.080 --> 00:32:58.820
This actually happened
to me once where

00:32:58.820 --> 00:33:00.920
I had a small
application-- a demo app,

00:33:00.920 --> 00:33:03.650
and every time I
demoed it, it was fine.

00:33:03.650 --> 00:33:06.510
When a product manager was
on stage demoing this app,

00:33:06.510 --> 00:33:07.430
it errored out.

00:33:07.430 --> 00:33:09.830
I was like, what happened--
what just happened here?

00:33:09.830 --> 00:33:12.877
And I couldn't figure this
out until I actually went

00:33:12.877 --> 00:33:14.460
to the instance and
used his computer,

00:33:14.460 --> 00:33:16.460
retriggered this,
and reproduced it,

00:33:16.460 --> 00:33:18.948
and then use the debuggered
to figure out, OK,

00:33:18.948 --> 00:33:20.990
so the app asks you for
the name and the message,

00:33:20.990 --> 00:33:23.110
and the person did
not enter the name.

00:33:23.110 --> 00:33:25.710
And of course, I did not have
error catching for this one

00:33:25.710 --> 00:33:27.920
because it's a demo app--
who would have known?

00:33:27.920 --> 00:33:29.870
It says very clearly
"enter your name."

00:33:29.870 --> 00:33:30.620
But who knew?

00:33:30.620 --> 00:33:33.740
And this was a real scenario
for a very small use case,

00:33:33.740 --> 00:33:35.110
and it definitely helped me.

00:33:35.110 --> 00:33:37.997
And this actually turned into
a pretty fun talk as well.

00:33:37.997 --> 00:33:39.830
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Users
